{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh3Hq7dVj1zHoqqTAzzT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DfiPeha2rJsTw6b5dZnCdv","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"grep -n \"§7\\.\\(1[0-9]\\|[0-9]\\)\" PLAN.md | head -60","description":"List section cross-references to verify numbering"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4313,"cache_read_input_tokens":390879,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4313},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f93111a9-5060-4eb2-b5bf-6a1585c61e13","uuid":"4577adb7-4373-41ee-8fa5-af153e3e8e8a","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:43:48.372Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh3Hpn3FhM7pmAtfUnEJ","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01DfiPeha2rJsTw6b5dZnCdv":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174755__x531263/work && grep -n \"§7\\.\\(1[0-9]\\|[0-9]\\)\" PLAN.md | head -60","description":"List section cross-references to verify numbering"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01DfiPeha2rJsTw6b5dZnCdv":{"cwd":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174755__x531263/work"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DfiPeha2rJsTw6b5dZnCdv","type":"tool_result","content":"49:| Audio | Everything is synthesised in code with the engine's chip synth, extended with instruments and effects (§6, §7.15–7.17). Voices are syllable-synth gibberish. |\n66:| Sun-drenched scenery that changes every minute (beaches, cities, mountains, canyons) | 15 hand-designed stages from 13 biome kits. Time of day runs from dawn to night as the trip goes west. The bloom lighting makes the sun and the sea glare. | §7.2, §8.2 |\n67:| A branching route: choose a road at each checkpoint, with several goals | A five-row pyramid of stages. The road physically forks into two (a new renderer feature). Each goal has its own ending in three moods. | §7.2, §8.2–8.3 |\n68:| Racing the clock, with checkpoints that extend the time | The **daylight clock**: a countdown, extended at each checkpoint gate. When it runs out, \"the sun sets\" and the run ends. | §7.7 |\n69:| Weaving through civilian traffic | Lane traffic of 11 vehicle types with lane changes. Near misses and overtakes score. | §7.5 |\n70:| The power slide: tap the brake while turning to drift at full speed | The drift model: a brake tap (classic), a quick down-up shift (manual), or the Drift button. Its angle, counter-steer and score are all modelled. | §7.4 |\n71:| Slipstreaming the car ahead | Slipstream builds behind any vehicle and gives a slingshot when you pull out. | §7.4 |\n72:| The passenger and her on-the-fly requests (Heart Attack) | **Love in Bloom**: three original passengers with 14 request types. You earn **petals**, which give a grade (E to AAA \"Full Bloom\"), and the grade sets the ending's mood. | §7.8, §8.7 |\n73:| The Coast 2 Coast career: missions from characters, miles, unlocks | The **Coast 2 Coast Tour**: 14 postcard missions from four characters, with drift duels, draft dashes, a knockout and a rival-couple duel. You earn **seeds** and plant them in the **Garage Garden** to unlock cars, songs, colours and outfits. | §7.9–7.10, §8.8–8.9 |\n74:| Choosing the song on the car radio before the race | A radio dial with 5 stations of original songs (plus 2 to unlock), switchable during the race. | §7.15, §8.10 |\n77:| Time attack, online racing | Time Trial with ghosts, local split-screen Versus, and AI Hub ghost-mode online races. | §7.11–7.13 |\n98:- **No copied UI.** No source logo, HUD layout or typography. Our HUD and menus are designed here (§7.18).\n172:  validated by the content-lint suite. Designers extend the game by editing these files; the dev tools (§7.23) are the\n334:6. **HUD** (§7.18), then dialogue bubbles, banners and toasts, then the debug overlay when it is on.\n465:  - **Slipstream source:** the vehicle ahead, per §7.3.\n498:  2. A 3-2-1 countdown on the gantry, the passenger's line, then \"GO!\". This is the rev start of §7.3.\n512:  - **In Love in Bloom**, about 600 segments before it, the passenger states a preference (§7.7).\n894:  - **Radio:** see §7.14.\n898:  - **About & Credits:** paged (§7.25).\n901:  (`E.Shell`). Theme them with the sunset palette and the E8 sizes, and give the Help screen our keys and sections (§7.19).\n1134:left: Bloom at 20 s or more, Bud at 5 s or more, Wilt below. Love in Bloom picks it from the grade (§7.7).\n1327:Every song needs its named layers and a `sparkle` layer (§7.14).\n1461:| **M1 Road and feel** (the riskiest first) | E1/E2 road junctions and dressing; S1, S2 and S3 with real layouts and first-pass scenery; `CarSim` complete (§7.3); traffic (§7.4); the clock and checkpoints; the HUD core; the Orchidea model pre-rendered (layers and yaws); `OB.Brain` bot; the dev panel's **Tuning** tab and debug overlay | the physics suite passes (curve-hold targets, drift entry and exit, slipstream, bumps, spins, rails); the junction suite shows a seamless switch (≤ 1 px); the bot drives S1→S2 and S1→S3 inside the target times; a filmstrip of a drift through a 5-curve looks right |\n1462:| **M2 The Arcade loop** | all 15 stages and 5 goals as real data with the kit rules; the route graph; scoring and the Bloom chain; results and initials; first-pass ending vignettes; title → menu → garage → radio → race; the UI kit with glyph prompts; settings (§7.20) with E5; the profile save; the synth extension E6/E7 with 2 radio songs, the engine note and core sound effects; E3 analog input | the 16-route playthrough passes (the bot reaches every goal in time on Normal); UI navigation passes for pad, keys and mouse; the glyph suite passes; content lint passes for the stages |\n1463:| **M3 Platform features** | AI Hub ghost session with our card (§7.12); attract mode (§7.22); touch-only (§7.21); every Dev Mode tab (§7.23) in the pause menu; local Versus split screen (§7.11); E4, E8 | the fake-hub suite and the **harness in a browser** pass (`__hubCheck.pass`, 2 and 4 players, latency and loss); the attract suite passes; touch emulation shows the notice; the dev suite passes (slider changes behaviour, reset restores it); two Versus bots finish |\n1466:| **M6 Audio content** | all 7 radio songs, 4 themes, every jingle; the full sound-effect list (§7.15); per-car engine notes; ambience per kit; radio switching; ducking; tunnel reverb | audio lint (levels, clipping, silence) and the spectrum bands pass for every song; every sound-effect name in the data exists |\n1467:| **M7 Art and effects** | all 13 kits fully dressed; every landmark; all skies; bloom, flare, shimmer and weather (sun shower, petal storm, aurora, fireworks, shooting stars); every car, traffic vehicle, rig and request-object animation (§7.13) | the screenshot review of each stage at 1920×1080 is done and noted in the log; `tools/check.mjs` look notes are acted on; there are no flat empty roadsides |\n1483:| R1 | **The fork renderer and the seamless stage switch.** The road module draws one road, so junctions are new code in the hottest path. | Build it first (M1). Keep it simple with the flat-junction rule (§7.1), one shared height profile and per-branch lateral offsets. Unit-test the projection: both branch centres; the road centre and player's screen x before and after the switch within 1 px; the gore cushion pushes to the nearer side. Record a filmstrip of a fork at 250 km/h, and review frames 1–16 for pops. |\n1484:| R2 | **Drift and handling feel**, the heart of the game, tuned without hands on a pad. | Every constant is a live slider from M1. The physics suite asserts the design targets (§7.3: a 3 holdable flat out, a 4 needing a lift, a 6 drifted at about 85 %). **Balance windows**, asserted by the bot suite: on Normal, the reference bot finishes the all-left route with 15–30 s spare and the all-right route with 2–12 s. A \"sloppy\" bot (1 spin per stage) fails at least half the routes. A \"no-drift\" bot finishes no more than 6 of the 16. |\n1487:| R5 | **Audio quality, judged by builders who can't hear.** | Composition rules (§7.14), named instrument presets, and the offline render lint: in Playwright, `OfflineAudioContext` renders 12 s of each song through the real synth code. It asserts a peak under 0.98, RMS between 0.06 and 0.25, no silence over 1 s (except a designed rest), and band energy order (low < 250 Hz present, 1–4 kHz present). It also writes spectrogram PNGs to `.cache/audio/` to look at. |\n1577:| Genre and perspective that fit the source | §1, §7.2 | screens |\n1582:| Engine animation plus new animations | §7.13 | filmstrips |\n1583:| All audio in code; gibberish voices | §7.14–7.16 | `audio-data`, `audio-lint`, `voice` |\n1584:| AI Hub multiplayer and the game on its own | §7.11, §7.12 | `hub`, `hub-harness`, `versus` |\n1587:| Performance unlimited, so add detail | §7.2, §9 | — |\n1590:| Dev mode in the pause menu, with live sliders and reset | §7.23, Appendix A | `dev` |\n1591:| Attract mode after 10 s idle; any input exits; cheats allowed | §7.22 | `attract` |\n1593:| 10-foot, controller-first; keyboard and mouse native; hover focus; PlayStation glyphs (Xbox option); prompts follow the last device | §7.17–7.20, Appendices B–C | `ui`, `glyphs` |\n1594:| No dense web-style screens | §7.17 | `ui` readability |\n1595:| Touch-only: no touch controls, the notice and Settings, attract runs | §7.21 | `touch`, `touch-emulation` |\n1597:| Source Information and three feedback blocks in the reply and the About screen | §7.25 | `content` |\n1598:| `exit-interview.json` in the exact shape, parsed | §7.25, M9 | `bundle` (parses it, checks every key is non-empty) |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f93111a9-5060-4eb2-b5bf-6a1585c61e13","uuid":"a2916e87-36d1-4c22-95bf-111d1d7392f2","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:43:48.399Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"49:| Audio | Everything is synthesised in code with the engine's chip synth, extended with instruments and effects (§6, §7.15–7.17). Voices are syllable-synth gibberish. |\n66:| Sun-drenched scenery that changes every minute (beaches, cities, mountains, canyons) | 15 hand-designed stages from 13 biome kits. Time of day runs from dawn to night as the trip goes west. The bloom lighting makes the sun and the sea glare. | §7.2, §8.2 |\n67:| A branching route: choose a road at each checkpoint, with several goals | A five-row pyramid of stages. The road physically forks into two (a new renderer feature). Each goal has its own ending in three moods. | §7.2, §8.2–8.3 |\n68:| Racing the clock, with checkpoints that extend the time | The **daylight clock**: a countdown, extended at each checkpoint gate. When it runs out, \"the sun sets\" and the run ends. | §7.7 |\n69:| Weaving through civilian traffic | Lane traffic of 11 vehicle types with lane changes. Near misses and overtakes score. | §7.5 |\n70:| The power slide: tap the brake while turning to drift at full speed | The drift model: a brake tap (classic), a quick down-up shift (manual), or the Drift button. Its angle, counter-steer and score are all modelled. | §7.4 |\n71:| Slipstreaming the car ahead | Slipstream builds behind any vehicle and gives a slingshot when you pull out. | §7.4 |\n72:| The passenger and her on-the-fly requests (Heart Attack) | **Love in Bloom**: three original passengers with 14 request types. You earn **petals**, which give a grade (E to AAA \"Full Bloom\"), and the grade sets the ending's mood. | §7.8, §8.7 |\n73:| The Coast 2 Coast career: missions from characters, miles, unlocks | The **Coast 2 Coast Tour**: 14 postcard missions from four characters, with drift duels, draft dashes, a knockout and a rival-couple duel. You earn **seeds** and plant them in the **Garage Garden** to unlock cars, songs, colours and outfits. | §7.9–7.10, §8.8–8.9 |\n74:| Choosing the song on the car radio before the race | A radio dial with 5 stations of original songs (plus 2 to unlock), switchable during the race. | §7.15, §8.10 |\n77:| Time attack, online racing | Time Trial with ghosts, local split-screen Versus, and AI Hub ghost-mode online races. | §7.11–7.13 |\n98:- **No copied UI.** No source logo, HUD layout or typography. Our HUD and menus are designed here (§7.18).\n172:  validated by the content-lint suite. Designers extend the game by editing these files; the dev tools (§7.23) are the\n334:6. **HUD** (§7.18), then dialogue bubbles, banners and toasts, then the debug overlay when it is on.\n465:  - **Slipstream source:** the vehicle ahead, per §7.3.\n498:  2. A 3-2-1 countdown on the gantry, the passenger's line, then \"GO!\". This is the rev start of §7.3.\n512:  - **In Love in Bloom**, about 600 segments before it, the passenger states a preference (§7.7).\n894:  - **Radio:** see §7.14.\n898:  - **About & Credits:** paged (§7.25).\n901:  (`E.Shell`). Theme them with the sunset palette and the E8 sizes, and give the Help screen our keys and sections (§7.19).\n1134:left: Bloom at 20 s or more, Bud at 5 s or more, Wilt below. Love in Bloom picks it from the grade (§7.7).\n1327:Every song needs its named layers and a `sparkle` layer (§7.14).\n1461:| **M1 Road and feel** (the riskiest first) | E1/E2 road junctions and dressing; S1, S2 and S3 with real layouts and first-pass scenery; `CarSim` complete (§7.3); traffic (§7.4); the clock and checkpoints; the HUD core; the Orchidea model pre-rendered (layers and yaws); `OB.Brain` bot; the dev panel's **Tuning** tab and debug overlay | the physics suite passes (curve-hold targets, drift entry and exit, slipstream, bumps, spins, rails); the junction suite shows a seamless switch (≤ 1 px); the bot drives S1→S2 and S1→S3 inside the target times; a filmstrip of a drift through a 5-curve looks right |\n1462:| **M2 The Arcade loop** | all 15 stages and 5 goals as real data with the kit rules; the route graph; scoring and the Bloom chain; results and initials; first-pass ending vignettes; title → menu → garage → radio → race; the UI kit with glyph prompts; settings (§7.20) with E5; the profile save; the synth extension E6/E7 with 2 radio songs, the engine note and core sound effects; E3 analog input | the 16-route playthrough passes (the bot reaches every goal in time on Normal); UI navigation passes for pad, keys and mouse; the glyph suite passes; content lint passes for the stages |\n1463:| **M3 Platform features** | AI Hub ghost session with our card (§7.12); attract mode (§7.22); touch-only (§7.21); every Dev Mode tab (§7.23) in the pause menu; local Versus split screen (§7.11); E4, E8 | the fake-hub suite and the **harness in a browser** pass (`__hubCheck.pass`, 2 and 4 players, latency and loss); the attract suite passes; touch emulation shows the notice; the dev suite passes (slider changes behaviour, reset restores it); two Versus bots finish |\n1466:| **M6 Audio content** | all 7 radio songs, 4 themes, every jingle; the full sound-effect list (§7.15); per-car engine notes; ambience per kit; radio switching; ducking; tunnel reverb | audio lint (levels, clipping, silence) and the spectrum bands pass for every song; every sound-effect name in the data exists |\n1467:| **M7 Art and effects** | all 13 kits fully dressed; every landmark; all skies; bloom, flare, shimmer and weather (sun shower, petal storm, aurora, fireworks, shooting stars); every car, traffic vehicle, rig and request-object animation (§7.13) | the screenshot review of each stage at 1920×1080 is done and noted in the log; `tools/check.mjs` look notes are acted on; there are no flat empty roadsides |\n1483:| R1 | **The fork renderer and the seamless stage switch.** The road module draws one road, so junctions are new code in the hottest path. | Build it first (M1). Keep it simple with the flat-junction rule (§7.1), one shared height profile and per-branch lateral offsets. Unit-test the projection: both branch centres; the road centre and player's screen x before and after the switch within 1 px; the gore cushion pushes to the nearer side. Record a filmstrip of a fork at 250 km/h, and review frames 1–16 for pops. |\n1484:| R2 | **Drift and handling feel**, the heart of the game, tuned without hands on a pad. | Every constant is a live slider from M1. The physics suite asserts the design targets (§7.3: a 3 holdable flat out, a 4 needing a lift, a 6 drifted at about 85 %). **Balance windows**, asserted by the bot suite: on Normal, the reference bot finishes the all-left route with 15–30 s spare and the all-right route with 2–12 s. A \"sloppy\" bot (1 spin per stage) fails at least half the routes. A \"no-drift\" bot finishes no more than 6 of the 16. |\n1487:| R5 | **Audio quality, judged by builders who can't hear.** | Composition rules (§7.14), named instrument presets, and the offline render lint: in Playwright, `OfflineAudioContext` renders 12 s of each song through the real synth code. It asserts a peak under 0.98, RMS between 0.06 and 0.25, no silence over 1 s (except a designed rest), and band energy order (low < 250 Hz present, 1–4 kHz present). It also writes spectrogram PNGs to `.cache/audio/` to look at. |\n1577:| Genre and perspective that fit the source | §1, §7.2 | screens |\n1582:| Engine animation plus new animations | §7.13 | filmstrips |\n1583:| All audio in code; gibberish voices | §7.14–7.16 | `audio-data`, `audio-lint`, `voice` |\n1584:| AI Hub multiplayer and the game on its own | §7.11, §7.12 | `hub`, `hub-harness`, `versus` |\n1587:| Performance unlimited, so add detail | §7.2, §9 | — |\n1590:| Dev mode in the pause menu, with live sliders and reset | §7.23, Appendix A | `dev` |\n1591:| Attract mode after 10 s idle; any input exits; cheats allowed | §7.22 | `attract` |\n1593:| 10-foot, controller-first; keyboard and mouse native; hover focus; PlayStation glyphs (Xbox option); prompts follow the last device | §7.17–7.20, Appendices B–C | `ui`, `glyphs` |\n1594:| No dense web-style screens | §7.17 | `ui` readability |\n1595:| Touch-only: no touch controls, the notice and Settings, attract runs | §7.21 | `touch`, `touch-emulation` |\n1597:| Source Information and three feedback blocks in the reply and the About screen | §7.25 | `content` |\n1598:| `exit-interview.json` in the exact shape, parsed | §7.25, M9 | `bundle` (parses it, checks every key is non-empty) |","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01DfiPeha2rJsTw6b5dZnCdv","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
